## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable data lifecycle with multi-tenant governance and cost control; integrates Iceberg features, storage tiering, policy-driven retention, and query-layer masking.

## Key Concepts
- Iceberg time travel via snapshots
- Dynamic data tiering (hot/warm/cold)
- Per-tenant data masking
- Encryption at rest (KMS)
- Partition pruning and metadata caching

## Code Example
```javascript
function selectTier(tenant, ageDays) {
  const retention = (tenantRetention[tenant] || 365);
  if (ageDays < 30) return 'hot';
  if (ageDays < retention) return 'warm';
  return 'cold';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data freshness and mask correctness across tiers?
- How would you validate time travel remains accurate after tiering moves?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend streaming and batch processing with a practical ML feature store, including contracts, versioning, lineage, and schema evolution—common in large tech orgs.

## Key Concepts
- Lakehouse (Iceberg) with feature tables
- Versioned feature registry and per-record data contracts
- Real-time (Flink) vs batch (Spark) feature computation
- Idempotent upserts and late-data handling
- End-to-end data lineage and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateRecord(record, schema) {
  // naive contract: required keys and types
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(schema.fields)) {
    if (!(k in record)) return { ok: false, error: `missing ${k}` };
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return { ok: false, error: `${k} type` };
  }
  return { ok: true };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate feature namespaces for different teams and enforce RBAC?
- How would you test schema evolution with backward-compat migrations?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design scalable, multi-tenant streaming graph pipelines with strict data contracts, lineage, and compliance controls in modern lakehouse setups.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time streaming patterns: watermarking, lateness windows, fault tolerance
- Idempotent upserts in Iceberg with composite keys (tenant/device/neighbor)
- Schema evolution for nested attributes and backward compatibility
- Per-record lineage and auditability; GDPR delete handling via tombstones
- Multi-tenant isolation, access controls, and governance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo upsert for edge state
function upsertEdge(existing, incoming) {
  const key = `${incoming.tenant_id}:${incoming.device_id}:${incoming.neighbor_id}`;
  if (!existing || existing.version < incoming.version) return incoming;
  return existing;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test late-arrival handling across tenants?
- What monitoring would you implement to detect cross-tenant data leakage or drift?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a practical, beginner-friendly end-to-end data pipeline with per-record contracts, canonical schemas, late data handling, and basic data quality reconciliation.

## Key Concepts

- Per-record contracts
- Lightweight schema registry
- Canonical schema and partitioning
- Watermarking for late data
- Data reconciliation and basic validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight per-record contract check (pseudo)
function validate(record){
  if(!record) return false;
  const has=(k)=>record[k]!==undefined && record[k]!==null;
  return has('device_id') && has('event_ts') && has('metric');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate schema drift across regions?
- How would you adjust the test plan for larger spikes in regional feeds?
